# DANIEL — "Five More Minutes"
# Theme: not every good moment needs entertainment. Sometimes being quietly together is enough.
# Seed: var.plush (whale / dino / star) — Act I, the studio's ancient claw machine ("Mr. Kim").
# Record: RECORD 08 — four-frame photo-booth strip, Hongdae, a rainy July night two years ago.

=== daniel_intro
@show daniel laugh at=c
@cam wide
> In the corner of the lobby, half hidden behind a rubber plant that has outlived the studio, a claw machine has woken up with everything else.
> Its lights run pink, then green, then a furious shade of blue. Its music is a nursery rhyme played by a robot having a long day.
> Daniel has both hands flat on the glass, like it owes him money.
daniel: {{playerName}}! Perfect timing. You're my witness.
you: To what?
daniel: History. This machine has taken about ninety thousand won off me since I was fifteen.
daniel: I used to come here for the cheap practice room upstairs. Then I'd come down here and lose. Every time.
@show charlie laugh at=l
charlie: He named it. Tell them what you named it.
daniel[neutral]: …Mr. Kim.
charlie: Because it has "the grip of a man who has given up."
daniel[laugh]: It's accurate! It picks things up out of pity and drops them out of spite.
> Inside the glass, a drift of plush toys, most of them sun-bleached on one side from years of facing the window.
daniel[smile]: Okay. You pick. Whatever you pick, I win it for you. That's a promise. Legally binding.
* [playful] The blue whale with the faded smile.
    @var plush = "whale"
    daniel: The whale. Classic. He's been in there since before the studio closed. He's basically a local.
* [sincere] The green dinosaur missing one felt tooth.
    @var plush = "dino"
    daniel: The dinosaur! Missing a tooth. That's not a flaw, that's a personality.
* [quiet] The small yellow star, wedged upside down in the far corner.
    @var plush = "star"
    daniel: The star. Upside down. In the worst corner. You have taste *and* no mercy.
@sfx claw
> He feeds in a coin. Charlie drums on the side of the cabinet. The claw descends with enormous confidence.
> It closes around your pick. It lifts it two centimetres. It thinks about it.
> It lets go.
charlie[surprised]: MR. KIM.
daniel[laugh]: See? Spite! Pure spite! It's fine. I have until five a.m. I've had worse odds.
@hide charlie
daniel[thinking]: Hey. ECHO said it has something of mine, right? Record eight?
daniel: I think I know what it is. I used to mail this place photo-booth strips. One a year. Like a very weird Christmas card.
daniel: The last one got rained on. You can't really see anything. Just me, being a lot.
* [playful] "Let me try Mr. Kim."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel: Oh, you're a *competitor*. Love that. Go.
    > You drop the claw dead centre. It closes on air, with dignity.
    daniel[laugh]: Mr. Kim doesn't play favourites. That's the one nice thing about him.
* [sincere] "Why photo booths?"
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel: Four chances to get it right. And if you don't, it's still funny. You can't lose in a photo booth.
* [quiet] Watch him feed in another coin.
    @rel daniel trust+1
    > The claw is still swinging when he starts talking again — about the machine, the plant, the song. He fills the gap before it can open.
daniel[smile]: Come back later. I'm getting that thing for you if it takes all night. It might take all night.
@hub act1

=== daniel_intro_repeat
@show daniel laugh at=c
daniel: Status report. Mr. Kim: eleven. Me: zero. Charlie: somehow minus two.
daniel: Don't worry. I'm pacing myself. It's a marathon. A very expensive marathon.
@hub act1

=== daniel_door
@bg studio_hall t=fade
@show daniel smile at=c
@cam wide
> Door eight is the last one in the hall. Light leaks out from under it in bands — pink, cyan, a green that doesn't exist in nature.
> Daniel is already there, bouncing on his heels like a boxer before the bell.
daniel: Welcome, welcome, to Door Number Eight! Behind it: a brand-new car. Or a goat.
you: Which one is it?
daniel[laugh]: Honestly? I'm scared it's the goat.
> He laughs. It comes out half a beat too fast.
daniel: Real talk, though? I've walked up to this door six times tonight. Every time, I remembered something urgent. Like snacks.
daniel[laugh]: I've eaten a lot of snacks.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 08. Owner present. Consent required.
@textbox normal
daniel[thinking]: Yeah. Yeah, okay. You have it, ECHO. Consent. Big yes.
daniel: It's a photo of me in a box. How bad can it be? I've been in loads of boxes.
daniel[shy]: …Only, the last frame's the one I never really got. So if you came in with me, that'd be good.
daniel: That'd be really good, actually.
* [sincere] "Open it."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[smile]: Okay! After you. No— after me. It's my goat.
    @goto daniel_room
* [careful] "Not yet."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[laugh]: Oh, thank god. I mean— sure. Totally. A very normal amount of relief.
    daniel: It's not going anywhere. Well, it is, at five. But not *yet*.
    daniel[smile]: Go on. I'll stay here and guard the goat.
    @hub act2

=== daniel_room
@bg mem_arcade t=memory
@music daniel
@fx prism n=1
@amb arcade
@cam reveal
> The door opens on noise.
> Not loud, exactly. Layered. A coin pusher shushing. A rhythm game counting down to nobody. A crane game playing the same four notes it has played for fifteen years.
> The light is the colour of highlighters left in a warm pocket. Pink bleeding into cyan bleeding into green.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 08 — OWNER: DANIEL. TYPE: PHOTOGRAPH, FOUR FRAMES. DATE: JULY, TWO YEARS AGO. CONDITION: WATER DAMAGE. UNRESOLVED SUBJECTS: 1.
echo: Reconstruction includes eleven machines and four hundred and six distinct sounds. I have lowered the volume. You are welcome.
@textbox normal
@show daniel laugh at=c
daniel: No way. No *way*. This is it. This is exactly it.
daniel: Hongdae! The one next to the chicken place with the broken sign. That drum game eats coins. That claw machine is a liar.
daniel: And the dance machine — the left pad sticks, so you have to stomp like you're angry at it—
> He moves from machine to machine like a host showing people round a party that has already started.
> He touches every one of them. He doesn't stop at any of them.
daniel: I came here every Thursday that summer, after practice. Lose at the claw machine. Lose at the drum game. Go home happy.
daniel[laugh]: It's a very good system. Losing, but loudly.
@if rooms() == 0
    daniel[thinking]: ECHO says there's someone unresolved in here. One subject.
    daniel: My money's on a ghost. Arcades have the best ghosts. Unfinished business. High scores.
    > You don't say anything. There's no reason, yet, to think it's anyone you know.
@elif rooms() >= 4
    daniel[smile]: So. ECHO says there's an unresolved subject in here.
    daniel: I'm not saying it's you. I'm saying I'd be a little offended if it wasn't.
    daniel[laugh]: Honestly, at this point, you should be charging ECHO rent.
@else
    daniel[thinking]: So. "Unresolved subject."
    daniel: People keep coming out of their rooms and looking at you funny. Is it going to be you again?
    > You'd like to say no. You're not sure you can.
* [playful] "Last one to the dance machine buys snacks."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    > He's already running. He was always going to be already running.
    daniel[laugh]: The left pad sticks! That's not cheating, that's local knowledge!
* [sincere] "You look happy in here."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[surprised]: Do I? I mean— yeah. Yes. Arcades are the best. Nobody's ever bored in an arcade. That's the whole point of them.
* [quiet] Stand still and let the noise go past you.
    @rel daniel trust+1
    > It takes him a moment to notice you've stopped. When he does, he comes back — slower than he left.
    daniel[smile]: Too much? It's a lot. I'm aware it's a lot.
daniel: The booth's outside, by the doors. That's where the record is. Come on.
@hide all
@textbox memory
@amb rain
@fx rain n=0.9
@cam pan
> The arcade doors slide open. Outside, the street is black and silver and coming down in sheets.
> A young man pushes out through the doors with his hood half up, laughing at himself — a claw-machine loser, empty-handed.
> His hair is dark. It changes his whole face. He looks like someone doing an impression of Daniel and getting it mostly right.
@textbox normal
@show daniel surprised at=r
daniel: Oh no. The hair. Look at the *hair*.
* [playful] "You look like your own little brother."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[laugh]: Rude! Accurate, but rude. I bleached it two weeks later. Unrelated. Mostly unrelated.
* [sincere] "He looks tired."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[thinking]: …Yeah. That was a long month. Evaluations. I lost to that claw machine for forty minutes because I didn't want to go home and think.
* [quiet] Watch the younger one pull his hood up, and grin at nothing.
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[smile]: He's grinning because he's alone. I used to practise it. So it'd be ready.
@hide daniel
@textbox memory
> The rain hits him like a bucket. He makes a noise like a kettle and dives for the nearest dry thing.
@bg mem_booth t=cut
@sfx rain_hit
> A photo booth, under an awning that is doing its best. The curtain swings shut behind him.
> He feeds it a coin, because standing in a photo booth without paying feels like trespassing.
> The little screen lights up. FOUR FRAMES. SMILE!
> One second later, the curtain swings open again.
> A second figure, soaked through, made of soft white light. No face. Just the shape of someone else who also ran.
daniel: Oh— it's going. It started. Okay. Okay, pose, *pose*—
@sfx shutter
@flash
> FRAME ONE. Daniel alone in the frame. Peace sign, tongue out, one eyebrow up. Professional.
you: Sorry — sorry, is someone—
@sfx shutter
@flash
> FRAME TWO. Daniel, turned halfway round, mouth open. Beside him, the edge of a wet sleeve.
daniel: It's taking pictures. I paid. You're in my photos now.
you: Then I want a refund.
@sfx shutter
@flash
> FRAME THREE. Both of them bent double. Nobody is looking at the camera. Nobody could.
@sfx glitch
@fx glitch n=0.6
> The fourth flash never comes. The reconstruction shivers, runs like wet ink, and stops.
@textbox echo
echo: FRAME FOUR: WATER DAMAGE. SUBJECT FACES NOT RECOVERABLE. AUDIO: INCOMPLETE.
echo: I am sorry. I have tried this one many times.
@textbox normal
@show daniel surprised at=c
daniel: You tried? How many times?
@textbox echo
echo: Four hundred and twelve. The rain always wins.
@textbox normal
daniel[smile]: ECHO. Buddy. You could've just asked me what was in it.
@textbox echo
echo: I did not know you would remember. Most people do not look at the last frame.
@textbox normal
@fx prism n=0.3
@show daniel thinking at=c
@cam closeup daniel
> The booth stays, half-built, glowing faintly at the seams. Daniel is sitting on its little bench. There is room for one more, just.
daniel: Yeah. That's the one. That's the frame that washed out.
daniel: I ran home with the strip in my back pocket. In the rain. Like a genius.
* [sincere] "What was in the fourth frame?"
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel: Nothing. That's the weird part. We weren't doing anything.
    daniel: Just sitting. Both of us. Looking at the curtain.
* [playful] "Frame one. You posed on reflex."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[laugh]: Muscle memory! A camera goes off, my hand does *this*. I'd do it at a job interview.
* [careful] "We can stop here, if you want."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[smile]: No. No— thanks. But I want to see it. I've wanted to see it for two years.
    daniel: It's nice that you asked, though. Nobody asks me if I want to stop. They assume I don't.
daniel: This is where it happened. The four minutes. Right here.
daniel: Sit? There's room. Barely. It was built for one person and their ego.
> You sit. The bench is exactly as narrow as it looks. Your shoulder and his are not quite touching, the way strangers manage it on a bus.
> Above you, the rain drums on the booth's thin roof. Hundreds of small hands, all at once.
> You hear yourself say it before you decide to.
you: It sounds like applause.
> Daniel goes completely still.
> You didn't know he could do that.
daniel[surprised]: …Say that again.
you: The rain. It sounds like—
daniel: Like applause. Yeah. That's what they said. Right when it got loud.
daniel: And I said, "For me?" And they said—
you: "No. For the rain. It isn't doing anything. It's just raining."
> The words come out in the right order, from somewhere you'd forgotten you kept them.
> A wet sleeve. A stranger's elbow in your ribs. The smell of the chicken place through the gap in the curtain.
> A stranger with dark hair, laughing so hard he forgot to pose.
> You'd kept all of it. You just never filed it anywhere.
@if rooms() == 0
    daniel: …Oh.
    daniel[surprised]: Oh, it's *you*. The ghost is you. You're the wet sleeve.
@else
    daniel[laugh]: Okay. Okay! I called it. I called it ten minutes ago!
    daniel[shy]: It's still weird, though. Good weird. It's been you the whole night.
@sfx shutter
@flash
@fx memory n=0.6
> Somewhere in the reconstruction, the fourth flash finally goes off.
> The washed-out frame fills in. Two people on a bench. Nobody posing. A curtain, a sleeve, a shoulder.
@textbox echo
echo: FRAME FOUR: RECOVERED. UNRESOLVED SUBJECT: IDENTIFIED. Good evening, {{playerName}}. You were very wet.
@textbox normal
* [sincere] "It was me. I remember the booth."
    @rel daniel trust+1 closeness+1
    you: I was moving the next day. I'd walked round Hongdae all night so I'd remember it properly.
    you: Then it rained, and the only thing I remembered was the booth.
    daniel[smile]: That's the best review that booth has ever had.
* [playful] "Do I still owe you for the photos?"
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[laugh]: Four thousand won. Plus interest. Plus emotional damages, because I thought you were a ghost for two years.
* [quiet] Look at the fourth frame for a while before saying anything. {if daniel.closeness >= 1}
    @rel daniel closeness+1 romance+1
    > Two people on a bench, not posing. You'd forgotten you could look like that. Not doing anything. Not about to leave.
    > When you glance over, Daniel isn't looking at the frame. He's looking at you looking at it.
daniel: Where did you go? After. You said "thanks for the booth" and just— went. Into the rain. Like a movie.
you: Somewhere else. A different city. I moved a lot.
you: I was always the one who left. I got good at it.
daniel[thinking]: That's a bad thing to be good at.
you: It's a very useful thing to be good at.
daniel[smile]: Yeah. Those are the worst ones.
daniel[thinking]: Can I tell you something dumb?
daniel: I sent Studio 25 a strip every year from when I was fifteen. Five strips.
daniel: Every one of them is me doing too much. Tongue out. Fake crying. One year I brought a traffic cone.
@textbox echo
echo: Records 08-A through 08-D are also on file. The traffic cone was returned to the city of Seoul. I checked.
@textbox normal
daniel[laugh]: It was *borrowed*. It had a very nice evening.
daniel: And then the last one. Frame four. Me, sitting there, doing nothing, with a stranger.
daniel[shy]: It's the only one I ever looked at twice.
daniel: I don't really do that. Nothing. When it gets quiet, I start talking. You've noticed. Songha's noticed. Everyone's noticed.
daniel: Because quiet's where people check their phones. Then they check the time. Then it's "oh, it's late," and they go home.
daniel: Not in a sad way. That's just how nights end. When the fun runs out.
> You know that moment. You have been the one checking the time. More than once, you've been the first one to say "it's late."
daniel[neutral]: But it got quiet in there, and you didn't go. Not till the rain stopped.
> Not till the rain stopped. You remember that part now — the wet street, the thank-you tossed over your shoulder. You always did know exactly when to leave.
daniel: I think about that a lot. Not about you. Sorry. About the four minutes.
daniel: Four minutes of not saying anything, and it was the best part. I've never figured out why.
* [sincere] "You don't have to keep anyone entertained. Not me, anyway."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel: …You say that now. You haven't seen me run out of material.
    daniel[thinking]: It's bad. It's just me going "so…" and looking at the ceiling.
    you: I'd like to see that.
    daniel[laugh]: That's the scariest thing anyone's ever said to me.
* [playful] "The rain got a standing ovation for doing nothing. You could too."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[laugh]: Okay, that's a good line. You can't just *have* good lines. That's my job.
* [romantic] "I'm not leaving when the rain stops. Not this time." {if daniel.trust >= 2}
    @rel daniel romance+1 trust+1
    > He opens his mouth. Nothing comes out. It might be the first time all night.
    daniel[shy]: Okay. Wow. Okay. I'm going to need a second with that one.
> The rain keeps going. ECHO, it seems, is in no hurry.
daniel: So what now? There's air hockey out there. I'm really good at air hockey. I could let you win. Badly.
* [romantic] "Stay here. Just until the rain stops." {if daniel.closeness >= 2}
    @rel daniel romance+1
    daniel: …Yeah?
    daniel[smile]: Yeah. Okay.
    > He settles back against the booth wall. His shoulder meets yours, and this time neither of you shifts away.
* [romantic] "What do you want to do? Not what's fun. What you want." {if daniel.trust >= 2}
    @rel daniel romance+1 trust+1
    daniel[thinking]: What I *want*.
    daniel: …Honestly? This. Exactly this. Which is annoying, because it's not a game and I can't win it.
* [quiet] Listen to the rain.
    @rel daniel trust+1
    > You listen. After a while, so does he. It takes him about eleven seconds to start humming along, but it's a start.
> Outside the curtain, the reconstructed street shines. A scooter hisses past. Someone's umbrella turns inside out, and they swear in a very tired voice.
daniel[laugh]: I remember that guy. I remember that umbrella. Why do I remember that umbrella?
you: Because you weren't talking.
daniel[thinking]: …Huh. That's annoying. That's a really annoying answer.
daniel[smile]: Okay. Challenge. Thirty seconds, no talking. I've never done it. Charlie says I physically can't.
> He takes a big breath, like he's about to go underwater.
> Five seconds. Ten. His knee starts to bounce. Fourteen. He presses his lips together very hard.
* [playful] Count the seconds out loud, very slowly.
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel[laugh]: That's sabotage! You can't— okay. I lost. Nineteen seconds. Still a personal best.
* [quiet] Rest your hand flat on the bench between you. {if daniel.romance >= 1}
    @rel daniel romance+1
    > His knee stops bouncing.
    > After a moment he puts his own hand down beside yours. Not on it. Beside it. The edges of your little fingers just touching.
    > Thirty seconds come and go. Neither of you mentions it.
* [sincere] Let him lose. Smile when he does.
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel[laugh]: Twenty-two seconds! Twenty-two! Write that down, ECHO.
    @textbox echo
    echo: Noted.
    @textbox normal
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 08: RESOLVED. Recipient Daniel, the photograph is yours. I am sorry about the water.
@textbox normal
daniel[smile]: Don't be. The water's the best part.
daniel: If it hadn't rained, I'd never have gone in the booth. I'd have just gone home.
@amb arcade
@fx prism n=0.5
@cam wide
> The rain thins to a patter, and the arcade noise comes back in, politely, like someone knocking before entering.
> Daniel stands, stretches until his shoulders click, and holds the curtain open for you.
daniel[smile]: Hey. {{playerName}}.
daniel: Thanks for the booth.
> It's your line. The one you tossed over your shoulder two years ago, stepping out into the wet. He kept it all this time.
you: Anytime.
daniel[laugh]: That's *my* line! Unbelievable. You're stealing my lines now.
> He steps out first, into an arcade that is still loud, still bright, still eleven machines and four hundred and six sounds.
> He doesn't run to any of them. He waits for you by the door.
@flag room_daniel
@unlock memory:daniel
@autosave
@goto hub_after_room

=== daniel_hub
@if flag.room_daniel and not flag.daniel_postchat
    @show daniel smile at=c
    > Daniel is lying across two lobby chairs pushed together, holding his phone above his face. Not scrolling. Just holding it.
    daniel: Oh, hey. Hi. Don't mind me. I'm being normal.
    daniel: ECHO put the strip on my phone. I never gave it my number. I've decided not to think about that.
    daniel[laugh]: Frame four's fixed. You look so wet. Like, *drowned*. It's the best photo I own.
    @show hyunjun laugh at=l
    hyunjun: He won't show anyone! He showed Charlie. Charlie cried.
    @show charlie shy at=r
    charlie: I did not cry. I had rain in my eye. Emotional rain.
    @hide hyunjun charlie
    daniel: I sat still in there. On purpose. For a *while*. I'm going to tell everyone at every opportunity.
    daniel[thinking]: Can I ask you something, though?
    daniel: When you left — back then, in the rain. Did you look back at the booth?
    * [sincere] "Once. At the corner."
        @rel daniel trust+1
        daniel: …I was still in there. I stayed till the rain stopped completely. I felt stupid getting out.
        daniel[smile]: So we were both being stupid about the same booth. Nice. That's nice.
    * [playful] "No. I was too busy running."
        @rel daniel closeness+1
        daniel[laugh]: Fair. You ran like a deer. A wet, rude deer.
    * [romantic] "I'm looking now." {if daniel.romance >= 2}
        @rel daniel romance+1
        daniel[shy]: …Okay, you have to stop doing that. Saying things. In a lobby. In front of people.
        > He grins at the ceiling like it just told him a joke only he gets.
    daniel: Go on. There are more doors. I'm going to lie here and be quiet for— I'm going for two minutes.
    @flag daniel_postchat
@elif flag.room_daniel
    @show daniel smile at=c
    @if rooms() >= 7
        daniel: Almost everyone's done. Whatever happens next, happens next.
        daniel[laugh]: I'm not planning it. Look at me, not planning it.
    @else
        daniel: New record: one minute forty of doing nothing. Hanbi timed it.
        daniel: He said "good job" like I'd just learned to ride a bike.
@else
    @show daniel laugh at=c
    daniel: Mr. Kim update: the claw *touched* it. It touched it! That's basically a relationship.
    @show songha neutral at=l
    songha: He's spent eight thousand won since the doors opened. I'm keeping a log.
    daniel: He's keeping a log. Of course he's keeping a log.
    @hide songha
    daniel[thinking]: My door's the one at the end. With the lights. I've walked past it twice.
    daniel: I'll do it. I will. I just have to be in the mood to be looked at by myself.
    daniel[laugh]: Which is usually always. That's the weird part. Usually I love it.
@hub act2

=== daniel_route
@chapter route
@time 03:50
@var route = "daniel"
@bg studio_lobby t=fade
@music fun
@amb room
@cam wide
> Nobody has to tell you where Daniel went. Mr. Kim is making noise again.
> He's in the corner behind the rubber plant, a paper cup of coins in one hand and — this is new — Songha's notebook in the other.
@show daniel laugh at=c
daniel: You came! Great. Okay. Great. I have a plan.
daniel: Seventy minutes. Eleven things. I borrowed Songha's notebook, so it's official.
daniel: One: rematch with Mr. Kim. Two: ECHO does the arcade again, full volume. Three: air hockey, best of seven—
daniel: Four, drum game. Five, dance machine. Six, racing. Seven, basketball. Eight, the one with the hammer.
daniel: Ten: sunrise, obviously. Eleven is a secret.
you: Eleven things?
daniel: Eleven *good* things. Number nine is "fireworks," with a question mark. ECHO hasn't got back to me about the fireworks.
@textbox echo
echo: I have got back to you. The answer is no.
@textbox normal
daniel[laugh]: See, that's a maybe.
> His phone buzzes on top of the claw machine. Then again. Then four more times. He tilts the screen so you can see.
@textbox phone
@sfx phone
songha: Has anyone seen my notebook.
hyunjun: daniel has it!!! he's making a PLAN
charlie: wait what's happening
hanbi: Let them be.
@textbox normal
> Daniel laughs, and puts the phone face down on top of the claw machine like it's in time-out.
* [playful] "Eleven things. Let's go. Starting with Mr. Kim."
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    daniel: *Yes*. That's the energy. That is exactly the energy.
* [sincere] "You don't have to plan the whole hour for me."
    @rel daniel trust+1
    daniel: I know! I know. It's not for you. It's— okay, it's a little for you.
    daniel: It's mostly so nobody gets bored. Me. So I don't get bored. Let's go with that.
* [quiet] Take a coin out of his cup.
    @rel daniel closeness+1
    > He watches you do it. Something in his shoulders comes down about a centimetre.
@sfx claw
> Mr. Kim's lights cycle pink, green, murderous blue.
@if daniel.romance >= 4
    daniel[shy]: Okay. Full disclosure, before we start.
    > He unzips the pocket of his windbreaker, and out comes—
    @if var.plush == "whale"
        > —the blue whale, faded smile and all, a little squashed from being carried around.
    @elif var.plush == "dino"
        > —the green dinosaur, missing tooth and all, a little squashed from being carried around.
    @elif var.plush == "star"
        > —the yellow star, right way up for once, a little squashed from being carried around.
    @else
        > —the plush you pointed at hours ago, a little squashed from being carried around.
    daniel: I got it around the snack argument. First try. After about eleven thousand won of not-first tries.
    daniel: It's been in my pocket ever since. I kept waiting for a good moment to give it to you.
    daniel: There weren't any. They were all loud. So— here. In a not-good moment.
    you: This is a good moment.
    daniel[laugh]: It is now. Everything's a good moment when you're holding one of those.
@else
    daniel: Rematch. You drive. I'll… advise.
    > You take the joystick. The claw lurches out over the pile. Daniel leans in beside you, close enough that his windbreaker crackles against your sleeve.
    daniel: Left. Bit more. No, *your* left— okay, stop, stop, drop it—
